<BaseLayout title="Generic Plan Regression: Synthetic PostgreSQL Scenario — DatabaseDeveloper" description="An illustrative PostgreSQL scenario showing what evidence to compare when prepared-statement plans behave differently across tenants.">
  <PageHero eyebrow="Synthetic scenario" title="When one prepared query behaves like two different workloads." description="Consider a skewed multi-tenant workload. Here is how to test whether plan selection explains different latency across tenant groups; no client result is claimed." />
  <section><div class="container prose">
    <h2>Context</h2><p>A multi-tenant table holds 50 established tenants and a handful of small, new tenants. The application uses prepared statements.</p>
    <h2>Symptom</h2><p>Established tenants are fine, but the same endpoint takes half a second for small tenants, and throughput drops for everyone as slow calls hold pooled connections.</p>
    <p>The <a href="/sample-report">sample report</a> works through this scenario in a reproducible lab on PostgreSQL 17, with measured before and after numbers.</p>
    <h2>Investigation</h2><p>Compare representative plans and actual row counts for different tenant cohorts, then connect those plans back to workload frequency and total time.</p>
    <h2>Evidence that would test the hypothesis</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Capture the prepared statement and compare <code>EXPLAIN EXECUTE</code> for a small and a large tenant in a safe environment. Record whether the plan is generic or uses the supplied parameter values.</li>
      <li>Compare estimated and actual rows, buffers, and execution time for both cohorts. Include planning time when testing a change to plan behavior.</li>
      <li>Check how often each tenant cohort executes the statement. A faster outlier plan is useful only if the whole workload improves.</li>
      <li>Test candidate changes, such as statistics, indexing, query shape, or prepare behavior, against both cohorts before recommending one.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>PostgreSQL documents how <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-prepare.html">generic and custom plans work with prepared statements</a>.</p>
    <h2>Lesson</h2><p>The goal is not to declare “generic plans are bad.” It is to identify whether one plan choice is inappropriate for a meaningful parameter distribution and test the safest correction.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section><div class="container"><CTA /></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
